# Datasets

- Datasets are stored outside of the project tree.
- There are two datasets, referred to as the "Kaggle dataset" and the "Prometheus dataset".
- Both datasets are simulated using Monte-Carlo simulation softwares used in high-energy physics (since we can never know with certainty the true labels — such as the neutrino energy or direction — when looking at real events).
- The Kaggle dataset was generated by the IceCube collaboration using their "full" simulation, which is more realistic, while the Prometheus dataset was generated using the Prometheus simulation software, which is much simpler and does not represent the underlying physics as well.
- The (larger) Kaggle dataset contains 131.8 million simulated events, while the (smaller) Prometheus dataset currently contains only about 400000 events.
- Both datasets contain roughly the same features (`time`, `sensor_id`, `charge`, and either `auxiliary` or `is_signal` — which is its boolean negation), and they both contain targets for the neutrino direction (`azimuth` and `zenith`), but Prometheus contains additional targets such as the neutrino energy or flavour.
- They are divided in various sub-dataset, each consisting of many Parquet files.
- The relevant sub-dataset for Prometheus is in the pulses/ or memmaped_pulses/ [sic] subfolder.
- Since loading many Parquet files isn’t very efficient, both datasets have first been preprocessed and saved to memory-mapped files.
- The raw "Prometheus dataset" is located at `~/icecube_ice_public_parquet-v2/` and has the following tree structure (ranges are contiguous, with some files omitted for brevity):
